\section{carriers}{

A scale of 71 fuels in three nested frames,
\verb{group (8) > carrier (17) > fuel (71)}, key \code{fuel}, taken from the
\code{carriers} palette of the energypal package (its
\code{inst/extdata/palettes/carriers.yml}, which cites the IPCC 2006
Guidelines and the IEA World Energy Balances for its classification).
energypal remains the editable source of the names, hierarchy, labels
and aliases; the definition is a snapshot built by \code{data-raw/carriers.R},
which stops when the two disagree.

The three carriers energypal declares without subtypes (\code{Electricity},
\code{Heat}, \code{Other}) become their own fuel, so every carrier is reachable
from the atoms. Five names appear both as a group and as the carrier
inside it (\code{Nuclear}, \code{Electricity}, \code{Heat}, \code{Storage}, \code{Other}), and
\code{Hydrogen} is both a carrier (under \code{Synthetic}) and a fuel (under
\code{Storage}): every lookup names its frame.

\strong{Attributes} (source \code{"IPCC2006"}, 26 rows): the default net calorific
value \code{lhv} (kind \code{property}, GJ/t) and CO2 emission factor \code{ef_co2}
(kind \code{factor}, kg/TJ) of the IPCC 2006 Guidelines, Vol. 2 Ch. 1,
Tables 1.2 and 1.4, attached to the 13 fuels whose definition matches an
IPCC fuel exactly. Fuels without a matching IPCC entry carry no value;
\code{\link[=scale_attributes]{scale_attributes()}} reports them as \code{NA} rather than inventing one.

\strong{Aliases} (136 rows): energypal's alternative spellings (source
\code{"energypal"}) and a set of model commodity codes such as \code{GAS}, \code{LIG},
\code{ELC} (source \code{"commodity"}). \code{\link[=scale_match]{scale_match()}} resolves them.

\code{meta$labels} carries energypal's short and long labels per member;
\code{meta$energypal_version} and \code{meta$palette_version} record the snapshot.
Colours are not stored: join them from energypal by name.
